diagnosis_text:
No network fault exists. A thorough five-phase diagnostic sweep of the entire topology — covering L2 identity, IP addressing, ACL rules, OSPF/FRR control-plane health, traffic-control shaping, DNS resolution, HTTP service reachability, and resource pressure — found zero anomalies on any device.

Contrary to the helpdesk ticket, the server farm (10.200.0.0/24) is fully reachable from host_1_1_1_1 right now. The reachability probe showed 0% packet loss from host_1_1_1_1 to all tested web servers (10.200.0.3 and 10.200.0.5). The service_snapshot confirmed that host_1_1_1_1 correctly resolves all four web hostnames via DNS and receives HTTP 200 from every web server (web0.local through web3.local) with sub-5 ms response times.

Crucially, router_core_3 — which the ticket accuses of never waking up — is entirely healthy. Its FRR/OSPF daemon is running, vtysh is responsive, and it maintains three Full OSPF adjacencies (to router_core_1, router_core_2, and switch_server_access). It is actively forwarding traffic to the server farm via the 172.16.0.10/31 link to switch_server_access.

Root cause: There is no fault. The outage described in ticket #8814 is either self-resolving before this investigation began, was a transient condition with no remaining trace (no OSPF flap history, no carrier-change events, no error counters), or was misattributed. No corrective action is required. router_core_3 does not need to be restarted.
=== END ===